>> A2: Briefly describe how you implemented argument parsing.  How do
>> you arrange for the elements of argv[] to be in the right order?
>> How do you avoid overflowing the stack page?

The command line is parsed in two stages with different purposes. 1. process_execute() extracts only what it needs to identify the child,
and prepares the bookkeeping. 2. start_process() tokenises the full command line into argv[] and hands it to load().

process_execute() first copies the command line into a kernel page with strlcpy, then extracts only the first token into a 16 byte buffer
to use as the thread name. We then allocate exec_args and child_info to initialize the synchronization plumbing. We then initialize all
memebers of child_info (cp) and then set all the members in exec_args. We then pass exec_args in thread_create() because it accepts a single
void *aux. Without it, the child could not receive both fn_copy and cp. We then create the thread and the add it to the child's list.

start_process() receives the copy and tokenizes it with strtok_r(cmdline, " ", &save_ptr), filling argv[argc++] in forward order.

setup_stack() builds the initial user stack top to bottom from PHYS_BASE.
1. Push the argument strings in reverse order (argv[argc-1] first), so argv[0]'s string ends up at the lowest string address.
2. Align the stack pointer to a 4 byte boundary.
3. Push the NULL terminator for argv[].
4. Push the argument pointers in reverse order, so argv[0]'s pointer ends up at the lowest address of the pointer array.
5. Push argv.
6. Push argc.
7. Push a fake return address of 0, so _start sees a normal C call frame.
========================================================================
                      USER STACK MEMORY LAYOUT (echo hi)
========================================================================
   Address (Example)      |  Memory Data Content      | C Type / Meaning
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
 LOW ADDRESS (Moves down towards 0x00000000)
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbfffffd4             |  0x00000000               | uint32_t (Fake Return Address)
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbfffffd8             |  2                        | int argc (Argument Count)
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbfffffdc             |  0xbfffffe0               | char **argv (Pointer to argv array)
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbfffffe0             |  0xbffffff8               | char *argv[0] -> pointer to "echo"
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbfffffe4             |  0xbffffffd               | char *argv[1] -> pointer to "hi"
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbfffffe8             |  0x00000000               | char *argv[2] (Null Pointer Sentinel)
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbfffffec             |  0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00   | Word Alignment (4-byte Padding)
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbffffff8             |  'e', 'c', 'h', 'o', '\0' | argument string 0 ("echo")
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
   0xbffffffd             |  'h', 'i', '\0'           | argument string 1 ("hi")
--------------------------+---------------------------+-----------------
 HIGH ADDRESS (PHYS_BASE = 0xc0000000 Boundary Right Above)
========================================================================

To avoid overflowing the stack page, setup_stack checks (sp - size >= (uint8_t *) PHYS_BASE - PGSIZE) and returns false, which fails load(),
killing the child if load_sucess fails before every push. This guarantees no write ever falls below the single mapped stack page.

