>> B2: Describe how file descriptors are associated with open files.
>> Are file descriptors unique within the entire OS or just within a
>> single process?
Every struct thread carries a fixed array (struct fd_entry *fd_table[FD_MAX]), 
where FD_MAX is 128. Each non NULL slot points to a heap-allocated
struct fd_entry {
	enum fd_type;
	struct file *file;
	struct pipe *pipe;
};
where the fd_type enum are the following: FD_FILE, FD_PIPE_R, FD_PIPE_W.

When we call sys_open(), the kernel calls filesys_open(), allocates (using malloc()) an
fd_entry with fd_type as FD_FILE, scans fd_table from index 2 for the first NULL, 
installs the entry, and returns the index.

pipe() installs two fd_entries, one for reader and one writer, pointing at the same struct pipe.
get_entry(fd) returns fd_table and get_file(fd) returns the file only if fd_type == FD_FILE. 
FDs 0 and 1 are conventionally for std input nad std output, so no fd_entry is ever installed in those indices.
FDs are unique only within a single process: slot 3 in process A and slot 3 in process B are unrelated. Each table lives inside a different
struct thread. So isolation is automatic.

