>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.
Three primitives in syscall.c, all built on the byte-fault-fixup pair:

get_user(uaddr) / put_user(uaddr, b): it is inline assembly {movl $1f, %0; movzbl %1, %0; 1:}.
If the access faults, the CPU jumps to the fixup label and the function returns -1/false without ever taking a page fault in the kernel.

read_user_byte → get_user, wrapping any fault into sys_exit(-1). read_user_word composes four read_user_bytes into a
uint32_t. check_user_string(s) walks read_user_byte until it sees '\0'. Scalars on the syscall stack are read as read_user_word((uint32_t *)
f->esp + n).

check_user_buffer(buf, size, writable): it returns immediately on size == 0. Otherwise it first asserts is_user_vaddr(buf) and size ≤ PHYS_BASE - buf
for overflow check. It then walks pages, at the start of each page it does a get_user, and, if writable, a put_user writing back the same byte to prove
writability. Since Pintos pages are all or nothing, one successful get_user() at a page boundary proves the entire page is mapped

Bulk copies are always preceded by a check_user_buffer call and then use the pointer directly. The kernel can dereference user virtual
addresses because the process's page directory is active in CR3 during the syscall, and file_read/file_write/putbuf run under that pagedir.
Any fault in the check phase kills the process via sys_exit(-1). The actual bulk copy is guaranteed not to fault.

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data to
>> be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?
We dont call pagedir_get_page(). We just inspect one byte per page with
get_user() and the MMU does the page table walk.
4,096 bytes: 1 if the buffer starts exactly on a page boundary, 2 otherwise 
(it always inspects two pages when it is not aligned).
2 bytes: 1, or 2 if the two bytes straddle a page boundary.

We dont see much room for improvement. One per page is the minimum
that still checks every page. The naive version, checking every byte,
would be 4,096 probes for the full page case.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.
We named it sys_wait() in our implementation. sys_wait() calls process_wait(). 
process_execute() created a child_info for
the child and pushed it on the parent's child_list, so process_wait()
searches that list for the tid. If it is not there (not a child, or
already waited for) it returns -1. Otherwise it does sema_down() on
wait_sema, reads exit_status, removes the record from the list, frees
it and returns the status.

When a process ends, process_exit() prints "name: exit(n)", closes
its files, then sets exit_status and is_exited in its child_info and
does sema_up() on wait_sema. If the process was killed by an exception,
kill() in exception.c sets exit_status to -1 first, so wait returns -1.
Because the record is removed on the first wait, a second wait on the
same pid finds nothing and returns -1.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.
We pushed all error handling into the helper functions. They
call sys_exit(-1) when fails, so the code in each system call has no 
"if (bad) return" lines. A call like
sys_create() just starts with check_user_string(name) and then does
its work as if the pointer were fine. Arguments come through the ARG()
macro, which goes through read_user_word(), so even the stack pointer
and the syscall number are checked the same way.

Things must to: First, validate everything before
acquiring a lock or allocating anything, so when the process dies at a
check there is nothing of the system call to undo. Second, everything
a process owns is released in process_exit(), which every
termination path goes through (exit(), exceptions, failed load): open
fds, the executable, the child records and the page directory.

Example: write(fd, buf, n). The handler reads fd, buf and n with
read_user_word(), then sys_write() calls check_user_buffer(buf, n,
false). If buf is bad, we exit at that point with no lock held. Only
after that do we take filesys_lock (or the pipe lock) and copy. A
process can therefore never die holding filesys_lock because of a bad
pointer.

