>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?
We implemented child_info to have semaphore, load_sema that starts at 0 
and a load_success flag. After thread_create(), process_execute() calls 
sema_down() on load_sema. The child's start_process() runs load(), stores 
the result in load_success, and does sema_up(). The failure paths 
(bad command line, too many arguments, load() fails..) all go through one 
helper, load_failed(), which also does the sema_up(), so the parent cannot 
get stuck. If load_success is false, exec returns -1.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?
P waits before C exits: P blocks on wait_sema, C upped it in process_exit(). 
P waits after C exits: the semaphore is already 1 and
the exit status is stored in the child_info, which is still there, so
wait returns right away. In both cases P frees the child_info.

P exits without waiting: process_exit() goes through child_list and
calls release_child() on each record. If the child is already
exited, the record is freed. If not, the record is marked is_orphan.
When that child exits later, process_exit() sees is_orphan and frees
the record itself. The check of is_exited/is_orphan and the update of
them are done with interrupts disabled, so the two sides cannot both
decide to leave it for the other (a leak) or both free it.

The struct thread page is freed by thread_schedule_tail() as usual,
whatever the parent does. 
A special case is a failed exec: the parent
uses the same release_child() on it, because the child may or may not
have finished exiting yet.

