>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?
We chose a two-tier design because the syscall mix has two very different access shapes:

Scalars and strings of unknown length. A single byte fault fixup pair handles both without needing to know the length in advance, and the fixup catches every fault, null, kernel pointer, unmapped page, at the exact instruction that caused it.

Bulk buffers (read/write data, console output). Walking with get_user per byte would be 4096 times more expensive than necessary.
Instead, check_user_buffer walks page-by-page using one get_user per page. This is sufficient because Pintos pages are all or nothing,and then hands the raw pointer to file_read/file_write/putbuf. The kernel can safely dereference user virtual addresses during a syscall since the current thread's pagedir is active in CR3.

The alternative is to temporarily map user pages into kernel virtual space. This wasn't used because it requires bookkeeping to unmap and would complicate the process's pagedir for no gain. The pagedir swap already happens on every context switch, so user VAs are directly usable from the kernel during the syscall.


